  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This BMJ article has an early but visible scholarly footprint: OpenAlex records 5 citations, and the reuse graph shows 5 downstream works drawing on it. The citing record is still small, but it is enough to show that the piece is being used across adjacent fields, especially methods-oriented and health-related review writing.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in 2008 in BMJ, this article presents and reviews the basics of evidence-based medicine through the lens of reading and appraising research papers. Its abstract indicates a practical, teaching-oriented contribution: it helps readers navigate study types, literature searching, data presentation and analysis, and the distinction between concepts that are often confused in research appraisal, such as reliability versus validity and systematic reviews versus meta-analyses.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The downstream record shows modest but meaningful scholarly uptake. OpenAlex currently counts 5 citations, with citing works appearing in 2011, 2019, 2024, and 2025. That pattern suggests an intermittent, continuing use rather than a burst of attention concentrated in a single period. The citing venues are varied and include software engineering, public health, gynecology and infertility research, and a review of ethical issues in open-label placebo research, indicating that the article is being used as a general methods or evidence-appraisal reference beyond its immediate medical context.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The reuse graph reinforces that interpretation: 5 downstream works are documented as reusing the article, split between articles and reviews. The cited contexts are particularly notable for their methodological emphasis, including systematic review practice, research education, and appraisal of evidence. In other words, the article appears to function less as a topic-specific source and more as a durable guide to evidence-based reading and review methods.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Other signals are more limited. Scite currently reports 2 mentioning citations and no supporting or contradicting counts, and event-data based public mentions are unavailable in this record. The article is also represented in repositories through an open version, which supports access and teaching use. Taken together, the evidence points to a compact but credible influence profile: a practical BMJ guide with cross-disciplinary uptake, modest citation volume, and signs of instructional and methodological reuse.</p>",
